# Card

`HmdCard` is the HQL type of a HyperMarkDown card. `Card` is the short spelling
used throughout these pages; both name the same type. It is the language-facing
projection of one `.hmd` file, supplied by the host adapter described in
[[hmd-integration]]. Nothing here is implemented by the bootstrap evaluator.

A card is the fundamental unit of HyperMarkDown: one file that is still valid
CommonMark, holding an optional YAML frontmatter block followed by body content.
HQL adopts that definition rather than inventing a document model of its own.

A card is a header and a document:

```hql
type HmdCard {
    header : Data
    doc    : Doc
}
```

The header is everything said *about* the card. The [[doc-type|doc]] is what was
written *in* it — body text, headings, anchors, links and embeds.

## Card is not a Doc

`HmdCard` and `Doc` are two types, composed. A card is not a subtype of a
document and does not inherit from one.

The temptation is real, because a card obviously has a body and a document
obviously has a body, so the body looks inherited. It is not. `card.body` is a
reacher: it reaches through the card to the document it holds.

```hql
card.body        // reaches card.doc.body
card.doc.body    // the same value, said explicitly
```

Nothing is gained by making that a subtype relation, and something is lost.
Subtyping would say a card may be used wherever a document is expected, which is
false in the direction that matters: a document has no header, no identity in
the vault, and nothing for [[data-type|Data]] to attach to. Keeping the two
types separate keeps that boundary honest, and keeps `Doc` reusable for content
that is not a card at all — an embedded fragment, a constructed body, a Markdown
file that was never given a header.

Which reachers exist is open. `body` is the obvious one; whether `sections`,
`links`, `uplinks` and `downlinks` reach through as well, or are reached through
`card.doc`, is a convenience decision, not a semantic one.

## The header is Data

The header is a [[data-type|Data]] tree, for the reason that card metadata is
deliberately open. HyperMarkDown reserves exactly four frontmatter keys — `tags`,
`use`, `import` and `nav` — and leaves every other key to the author. A type
system that declared a fixed `Frontmatter` record would have to grow a field
whenever a vault invented a key.

```hql
card.header : Data
```

`header` is HQL's name, and it is deliberately not `frontmatter`. Frontmatter is
HMD's name for a block of YAML at the top of a file; the header is the card's
metadata whatever its origin, and extensions contribute to it without writing
YAML anywhere. Frontmatter is the canonical source of a header, not its
definition.

That is also why the header is not optional. A card with no frontmatter block
has an empty header rather than no header, so there is no `Data?` to unwrap
before every lookup and no difference in kind between a card that was authored
without YAML and one whose YAML happened to be empty. A missing *key* is still
a missing key; that is a question for path traversal, not for the header itself.

Traversal is path navigation through `Data`, not field access on a declared
record:

```hql
card.header.status
card.header.nav.visibility
```

Reserved keys are the part of that tree whose meaning the toolchain already
fixes. `tags` is a list of `/`-separated hierarchical strings that categorize a
card without participating in resolution. `use` toggles per-card features and
inherits from the nearest ancestor folder note. `import` binds names and adds
search origins for that card only, and does not inherit. `nav` carries `order`
and `visibility` for publication. Projecting these as stronger HQL types is
sound because HMD validates them; the rest of the tree stays `Data`.

What a card whose frontmatter fails to parse projects to is unresolved — a card
with an empty header, a card carrying a diagnostic, or no card at all. So is
whether the authored frontmatter remains separately visible underneath a header
that extensions have added to.

## cards

`cards` is a reserved keyword. It denotes every card in the current namespace
vault:

```hql
cards : List<HmdCard>
```

This is a context value, not a function call and not a filesystem sweep written
by the user. The vault boundary is HMD's: a namespace is a rooted tree of
modules, and resolution never crosses into a neighbouring vault. `cards`
therefore has the same horizon that a wikilink does — two vaults are two
namespaces, and `cards` in one never contains a card from the other.

```hql
cards
| typed Relation
| graph
```

Being a keyword rather than a binding means a card cannot shadow it by being
named `cards`, and that a program never has to state where the vault is. What
`cards` means inside a nested module — the whole vault, or the module subtree —
still needs a decision, as does whether imported namespaces contribute to it.

> The collection type is unsettled. This card states `List<HmdCard>`, while
> [[hql/collections]] records `Set<Card>` as the direction that supersedes the
> older corpus `List[Card]`, on the grounds that discovering files must not
> establish an ordering guarantee. Both spellings are currently in the wiki;
> the two pages have to be reconciled by a decision about whether vault order
> is observable, not by editing one page to match the other.

## Extensions extend the header

The header is the extension boundary. An [[hql/extensions|extension]] that knows
something about a card contributes it to the header, without the HQL core
learning a new document type.

Contributed metadata can come from outside the file entirely — a `git`
extension supplying authorship and revision, an indexer supplying embeddings or
a semantic-search score, a link checker supplying resolution diagnostics, a
build supplying publication state. None of these are frontmatter, and none of
them require the author to write anything.

```hql
git.metadata(card) : Data
```

This is the second reason the header is broader than frontmatter: most of what
a mature vault knows about a card was never typed into the card.

The core's obligation is to keep these contributions distinguishable. A header
entry asserted by the author, derived by an extension, or taken from an ancestor
folder note is not interchangeable with the others, and provenance records where a value came
from rather than conferring authority on it. How contributed metadata is
namespaced, whether an extension may overwrite an author's key, and what happens
when two extensions contribute the same path are open.

## Types narrow

The consequence of an open header is that card types get *narrowed* rather than
declared up front. Every card is an `HmdCard`. Some cards additionally satisfy a
stronger contract, and that contract is usually evidenced by the header.

```hql
type Relation[S, T] <: HmdCard {
    source: Ref[S]
    target: Ref[T]
}

type WorksOn <: Relation[Person, Project] {
    since: Date?
    role: Role?
}
```

Declaration syntax is illustrative. The direction is not: a Relation Card is a
card, so it keeps its body, its links and its own identity, and other cards can
link to it. Narrowing adds guarantees; it never replaces the card with a record.

This is a card-to-card relation and is the one place subtyping earns its keep:
a `WorksOn` genuinely may be used wherever an `HmdCard` is expected. It is not
the relation between `HmdCard` and `Doc`, which is composition.
See [[hql/knowledge]] for why significant relationships are preferably cards.

Narrowing has to be checked. A `type: WorksOn` entry in the header is an
author's claim, and `typed Relation` must verify the contract rather than trust
the label — an unchecked `as` would let any YAML key mint a type. The
result of a failed narrowing, the spelling of validation and refinement, and
whether narrowing is nominal or structural remain open.

```hql
person : Person = validate[Person](card)
```

Narrowing is also how a vault stays usable while it is being typed. A card that
satisfies nothing beyond `HmdCard` is still a first-class value, still in
`cards`, still traversable. Stronger types are earned where there is a reason
for them, and the untyped remainder is not an error.

See [[doc-type]] for the document a card holds, [[data-type]] for the header's
tree type, [[pipes]] for composition and [[hql/design-status]] for what is still
undecided.


