Yes. That changes the model in an important way.

`card.frontmatter` should be the raw, authored YAML metadata from the beginning of the `.hmd` file:

```hql
card.frontmatter : Data?
```

`card.metadata` is the effective metadata tree assembled for the card:

```hql
card.metadata : Data
```

Conceptually:

```text
system metadata
+ extension metadata
+ frontmatter
----------------
card.metadata
```

with frontmatter taking precedence where the same path exists.

That means `metadata` is not just another name for frontmatter. It is the runtime HQL view of everything known about the card.

The reserved `.hmd/**` vault state fits naturally into that model. Things persisted by the toolchain, rather than authored in the card, can appear under something like:

```hql
card.metadata.state
```

So semantic-search machinery can maintain state such as embeddings, L0/L1 summaries, hashes, index versions, timestamps, or whatever else we settle on inside the vault's reserved `.hmd/` storage, while HQL sees it through the card:

```hql
card.metadata.state.semantic
```

The physical storage location and the logical HQL tree are separate concerns. `.hmd/**` is where the implementation persists it; `card.metadata.state` is how HQL sees it.

This also makes the refinement idea cleaner. `metadata` begins as a `Data` tree, but parts of it progressively acquire stronger types because HMD core and loaded extensions know what those paths mean.

For example, HQL may initially know only:

```hql
card.metadata.state : Data
```

After the semantic-search extension is loaded, the same subtree might be known as:

```text
SemanticSearchState
```

without converting the underlying data into a different object. The extension contributes type knowledge about the tree.

That is the mechanism we were calling "rigorous duck typing": really an open structural value with progressively stronger typing.

And yes, this also makes your relation idea more interesting:

```text
Relation[S,T] <: Card
WorksOn <: Relation[Person,Project]
```

Treating that notation as illustrative only, the **semantic idea is strong**.

A relation can itself be a card.

That means a relation is not merely an anonymous graph edge:

```text
Alice --worksOn--> HQL
```

It can have everything a card has: authored text, metadata, evidence, provenance, links, state, and potentially its own semantic relationships.

Conceptually:

```text
Card
  ↑
Relation<Person, Project>
  ↑
WorksOn
```

But I would be careful about describing this as classical OO inheritance.

`Relation<Person,Project>` is probably better understood as a **refinement of `Card`**: a card whose semantic structure establishes a source of type `Person`, a target of type `Project`, and relation semantics.

So a `WorksOn` card might effectively satisfy constraints like:

```text
source : Person
target : Project
relation : WorksOn
```

while still being an ordinary HMD card underneath.

This gives a very elegant unification:

```text
cards
    : List<Card>

some cards refine to Person
some cards refine to Project
some cards refine to Relation<Person,Project>
some of those refine further to WorksOn
```

You don't need separate universes of "documents", "entities", and "edges". They are increasingly typed interpretations of cards.

And that fits the metadata story almost perfectly: **a card starts as document + open metadata; HMD and HQL extensions progressively establish what kind of thing that card is.**
